FINDINGS:

1. [MAJOR] src/runtime.rs:2643 — `collections.push_left`/`push_right` enforce the item cap only by re-running `deque_parts` on the updated value, but no test exercises that refusal; `tests/collections_test.rs:900` tests only the constructor. SPEC.md's new promise is "the constructor **and every push** refuse a larger one", and the REPORT's `resource_limit` matrix names only the constructor-budget refusal. So the push-at-cap branch (`src/runtime.rs:1955`, reached via 2643/2656) has no test on either side. Add a test that fills a deque to `MAX_DEQUE_ITEMS` and asserts the next push is refused, and one that a forged record already at the cap is refused. — VERIFIED: read the full test file (lines 1–120, 320–927) and the REPORT's test table; neither contains a push-at-cap case.

2. [MINOR] src/runtime.rs:1892 — `deque_error` always says `"a value made by collections.deque"`, so the constructor refusal at `src/runtime.rs:2624` tells a user who passed a **list of 1,000,001 items** that their value is not a deque, and a push at the cap says the same. The message does not name the `MAX_DEQUE_ITEMS` limit or the actual length, so it is not actionable. `tests/collections_test.rs:908` pins exactly this unhelpful substring. Use a dedicated budget error that names the limit and the supplied length (and keep `deque_error` for malformed values).

3. [MINOR] src/runtime.rs:1918 — the trusted `Value::Object` branch trusts any object whose name is `"deque"` without checking its `kind` field, while the record branch at 1928–1932 requires `kind == "deque"`. Trust is therefore keyed on an object name alone. Today only `deque_value` (line 1905) constructs such an object, so it is not reachable from source, but any future builtin returning `Value::object("deque", …)` would be silently trusted and skip `validate_stack_tree`. Require the `kind` field on the object path too (or make the trust marker a field only `deque_value` sets and check it).

